 ## Stop, ticker and heartbeat (`stop`, `ticker`)
 
-`stop::StopSource` is the one trigger for a host's stop: `stop_on_signals` (SIGINT and SIGTERM on Unix, the console control handler on Windows; a 50 ms watcher thread turns the signal into a trigger) and `stop_on_stdin_close` both feed it, and `StopToken` is what the host's threads wait on. `ticker::spawn_ticker` runs a closure every interval until the stop, and `ticker::spawn_heartbeat` writes a `{pid, updated_ms, state}` record atomically to a path on the same schedule.
+`stop::StopSource` is the one trigger for a host's stop: `stop_on_signals` (SIGINT and SIGTERM on Unix, the console control handler on Windows; a 50 ms watcher thread turns the signal into a trigger) and `stop_on_stdin_close` both feed it, and `StopToken` is what the host's threads wait on. `stop_on_parent_exit(source, pid)` triggers it when a named process (the parent) is gone (cause `Requested`), and `at_exit(hook)` runs a closure when the process ends normally. `ticker::spawn_ticker` runs a closure every interval until the stop, and `ticker::spawn_heartbeat` writes a `{pid, updated_ms, state}` record atomically to a path on the same schedule.
+
+## Wake signal (`wake`)
+
+`wake::Wake::new(&suite, service, name)` is a cross-process "look again" hint: `post()` in one process, `listen()` then `wait(timeout)` in another. macOS uses a Darwin notification, Windows a named auto-reset event with the owner-only DACL, other platforms a counter file in the suite's private `wake` directory. A wake carries no data and is not authenticated on macOS; the receiver re-reads its state and keeps a periodic re-check. Posts coalesce.
+
+## Background program install (`install`)
+
+`install::ServiceSpec` (name, absolute program, arguments, environment, restart policy) plus `ServiceManager::{install, start, stop, status, uninstall}` for `systemd --user` (`SystemdUser`), a launchd LaunchAgent (`LaunchAgent`) and a Task Scheduler logon task (`ScheduledTask`). Each OS tool runs through a replaceable `CommandRunner`, and the unit, plist and task XML come from pure `render_*` functions. A value that cannot be written safely is refused; a task with environment entries is refused (a task has none). Windows services and Authenticode verification are not built.
+
+## Current-user SID (Windows)
+
+`current_user_sid_string()` returns the process token's SID string, for naming a per-user pipe.
 
